<http://example.surroundaustralia.com/ran/jmap/planning/> {
    jmap:MissionPlanComponent a owl:Class ;
      rdfs:label "JMAP Mission Plan Component" ;
      rdfs:comment "Enables inferencential reference to planning components for mission instance" .
    jmap:MissionAnalysisInput a owl:Class , <https://surroundaustralia.com/demo/routeplanner/Dataset> ;
      rdfs:label "Mission Analysis Input Dataset" .
    jmap:UavRouteData a jmap:MissionAnalysisInput , <https://surroundaustralia.com/demo/routeplanner/Dataset> , owl:NamedIndividual ;
      rdfs:label "UAV Route Dataset" .
    jmap:MissionScope a owl:Class , jmap:MissionPlanComponent ;
      rdfs:label "Mission Scope" ;
      rdfs:comment """a. examine requirements within commander’s initial guidance (CCIR, risk, information on the situation)

b. seek input from previous planning activities

c. establish timelines.""" ;
      jmap:hasGlossaryReference jmap_abbr:ccir , jmap_abbr:jipoe , jmap_abbr:oplan , jmap_terms:alertorderalerto , jmap_terms:commanderscriticalinformationrequirementsccir , jmap_terms:jointintelligencepreparationoftheoperationalenvironmentjipoe , jmap_terms:operationplanoplan ;
      jmap:hasPlanningInput jmap:MissionAnalysis .
    jmap:hasGlossaryReference a owl:ObjectProperty ;
      rdfs:label "has Glossary Reference" ;
      rdfs:domain jmap:MissionPlanComponent ;
      rdfs:range :jmap_terms , :jmap_abbr .
    jmap:hasPlanningInput a owl:ObjectProperty ;
      rdfs:label "has Planning Input" ;
      rdfs:domain jmap:MissionPlanComponent ;
      rdfs:range jmap:MissionAnalysis .
    jmap:MissionAnalysis a owl:Class , jmap:MissionPlanComponent ;
      rdfs:label "Mission Analysis" ;
      rdfs:comment """Mission Analysis (MA) is the second step in the Joint Military Appreciation Process (JMAP).
In MA, the operational design work begun in Scoping and Framing is further developed until a thorough description of the commander’s operational approach is created through a schematic depicting a line or lines of operation (LOO) that together achieve the desired end state.

This step is the most substantial in terms of the breadth of issues considered and scope and detail of output generated. In common with Scoping and Framing, MA demands flexible, creative and critical thinking throughout to ensure the best possible planning outcomes.
Whenever possible, planning staff should seek to capture a narrative or account of the logic behind all end states, objectives, assumptions, operational analysis, conclusions, effects and their desired outcomes. This provides the process with greater clarity, authenticity, accountability and transparency as it develops.

Such a narrative also assists in formulating effective risk and assessment strategies to be employed once the plan is executed.""" ;
      jmap:hasGlossaryReference jmap_abbr:cdf , jmap_abbr:jipoe , jmap_terms:jointintelligencepreparationoftheoperationalenvironmentjipoe ;
      jmap:hasAnalysisInput jmap:UavRouteData .
    jmap:MissionFrame a owl:Class , jmap:MissionPlanComponent ;
      rdfs:label "Mission Frame" ;
      rdfs:comment """a. environment frame:
(1) how the OE developed from a historical and cultural perspective, how it currently exists (current conditions), possible future conditions, and how these relate to the desired strategic end state

(2) own capabilities and current operational commitment

(3) identifying assumptions about systems in the OE to enable rapid adaptation to change within it

(4) which actors exist within the OE, along with their identity, history, culture, current state and future goals, and the nature of relationships between actors

(5) the strategic intent of any threat, including its objectives, limitations, specific direction and time constraints

(6) causes of conflict within the OE and between actors (which may be historical, economic, ethnic, sectarian)

(7) physical conditions within the OE and their implications for operations (which may include major terrain features, major infrastructure and weather).

b. problem frame:
(1) strategic level direction

(2) answers ‘why has the problem arisen, which conditions and actors are oppositional and which supportive, what organisation challenges exist, what broad resources are available?

(3) status of current operations

(4) the commander’s initial guidance, including time constraints and planning considerations, force preparation and capability requirements, and guidance from previous planning

(5) intelligence and CCIR updates.
""" ;
      jmap:hasGlossaryReference jmap_abbr:ccir , jmap_abbr:oe , jmap_terms:assumption , jmap_terms:commanderscriticalinformationrequirementsccir ;
      jmap:hasPlanningInput jmap:MissionAnalysis .
    jmap:hasAnalysisInput a owl:ObjectProperty ;
      rdfs:label "has Analysis Input" ;
      rdfs:domain jmap:MissionAnalysis ;
      rdfs:range jmap:MissionAnalysisInput .
}

